While loops are commonly used in programming when a certain block of code needs to be executed repeatedly until a specific condition is met. The primary purpose of while loops is to control flow and repeat a set of instructions until a certain condition is satisfied. 
One of the potential impacts of while loops on computational efficiency is that they may cause an infinite loop if the condition is not properly defined or if the loop takes too long to complete. This can cause the program to crash or become unresponsive, which can be a major issue in some contexts.
While loops can interact with other programming constructs, such as if statements and for loops, to create more complex control structures. For example, a while loop can be nested inside an if statement to create a conditional loop that only executes when a certain condition is met. Similarly, a for loop can be used to iterate over a specific range of values, while a while loop can be used to repeat a set of instructions until a certain condition is met.
Here are some specific examples of while loops in different programming languages:
Python:
```
i = 0
while i < 5:
    print(i)
    i += 1
```
This code will print the numbers 0 through 4, since the loop will repeat until i is no longer less than 5.
Java:
```
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}
```
This code is very similar to the Python example, but with Java syntax.
C++:
```
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    cout << i << endl;
    i++;
}
```
This code is also very similar, but with C++ syntax.
While loops can be compared and contrasted with other control structures like for loops and if statements. For loops are typically used when the number of iterations is known in advance, while while loops are used when the number of iterations is not known in advance. If statements are used to execute code conditionally, while while loops are used to execute code repeatedly until a certain condition is met.
In some cases, using a while loop may be less efficient than using an alternative control structure. For example, if the number of iterations is known in advance, a for loop may be more efficient since it does not require the overhead of checking a condition on each iteration. However, in other cases, a while loop may be the most appropriate control structure, such as when waiting for user input or reading data from a file. Ultimately, the choice of control structure will depend on the specific requirements of the program and the context in which it is being used.

