One way to sort an array of integers in descending order without using any built-in sorting functions or libraries is to implement a variation of the bubble sort algorithm.
Here's how you can modify your C++ code to efficiently sort an array of integers in descending order using bubble sort:
```
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[j + 1]) {
                // swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we loop through the array `n-1` times, comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. The `if` condition checks if `arr[j]` is less than `arr[j+1]`, which means they are in the wrong order and need to be swapped. By swapping them, we ensure that the largest element "bubbles up" to the top of the array.
With this implementation, the largest element will eventually "bubble up" to the first position of the array, and the array will be sorted in descending order.
You can call this function in your main program like this:
```
int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This will output: `9 6 5 5 2 1`.

