To optimize the code and minimize the amount of memory used when parsing and accessing the JSON data, one approach is to use a streaming parser instead of a DOM-style parser. A streaming parser reads the JSON data one token at a time, which can be more memory-efficient than loading the entire JSON data into memory. 
Here's an example of how this can be implemented using the `Json::CharReaderBuilder` class:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <jsoncpp/json/json.h>
class HeartParser : public Json::CharReader {
public:
    HeartParser() {}
    ~HeartParser() {}
    bool parse(const char* json, size_t length) {
        Json::CharReaderBuilder builder;
        builder["collectComments"] = false;
        return Json::parseFromStream(builder, Json::CharReaderBuilder::makeDefaultSettings(), stream(json, length), &root_);
    }
    Json::Value root() const {
        return root_;
    }
private:
    class stream : public std::istringstream {
    public:
        stream(const char* json, size_t length) : std::istringstream(std::string(json, length)) {}
        ~stream() {}
    };
    Json::Value root_;
};
int main() {
    std::string json_data = R"({
        "location": "Chest",
        "description": "A muscular organ that pumps blood through the circulatory system"
    })";
    HeartParser parser;
    if (!parser.parse(json_data.c_str(), json_data.size())) {
        std::cout << "Failed to parse JSON data" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }
    try {
        std::string location = parser.root()["location"].asString();
        std::string description = parser.root().get("description", "No description available").asString();
        std::cout << "Location: " << location << std::endl;
        std::cout << "Description: " << description << std::endl;
    }
    catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "Error: " << e.what() << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `HeartParser` class inherits from the `Json::CharReader` class and overrides its `parse()` method to use a streaming parser instead of a DOM-style parser. The `parse()` method takes a `const char*` pointer to the JSON data and its length as arguments, and returns a boolean indicating whether the parsing was successful. The `root()` method returns the parsed JSON data as a `Json::Value` object.
The `stream` class is a custom implementation of `std::istringstream` that takes a `const char*` pointer to the JSON data and its length as arguments, and converts it to a `std::string` object that can be passed to the streaming parser.
Overall, using a streaming parser can be more memory-efficient than using a DOM-style parser, especially for large JSON data sets. However, it may be less convenient to use since it requires more manual handling of the JSON data.

