\end{equation}
Multiplying the rate of heat transfer by the duration of immersion (30 minutes = 1800 seconds), we get:
\begin{equation}
Q = 6660 \text{ W} \times 1800 \text{ s} = 11,988,000 \text{ J}
\end{equation}
Finally, we can calculate the specific heat capacity of the body as:
\begin{equation}
c = \frac{11,988,000 \text{ J}}{60 \text{ kg} \times 37°C} \approx 455 \text{ J/kg°C}
\end{equation}
This means that the human body has a high specific heat capacity, which makes it difficult to change its temperature quickly. During a 30-minute immersion in icy cold water, the body would lose a significant amount of heat to the water, causing discomfort and potentially leading to hypothermia if not properly protected.

