How can we analyze the distribution of words in a given text using Go programming language?
One approach could be to use the bufio and strings packages in Go to read the text file, split it into words, and count their frequency. Here's some sample Go code:
package main
import (
    "bufio"
    "fmt"
    "os"
    "strings"
)
func main() {
    // open the text file
    file, err := os.Open("example.txt")
    if err != nil {
        panic(err)
    }
    defer file.Close()
    // create a scanner to read the file
    scanner := bufio.NewScanner(file)
    // set the split function for the scanner
    scanner.Split(bufio.ScanWords)
    // create a map to store the word frequency
    wordFreq := make(map[string]int)
    // loop through the scanner and count the word frequency
    for scanner.Scan() {
        word := strings.ToLower(scanner.Text())
        wordFreq[word]++
    }
    // print the word frequency
    for word, freq := range wordFreq {
        fmt.Printf("%s: %d\n", word, freq)
    }
}
This code will read a text file named "example.txt", split it into words, and count the frequency of each word. The result will be a map where the keys are the words and the values are the frequency of each word.

