Sure, here is the algorithm for bubble sorting an array in ascending order:
1. Start with the first element of the array (i=0) and compare it with the next element (i+1).
2. If the next element is smaller, swap the two elements.
3. Move to the next pair of elements and repeat step 2.
4. Continue this process until the end of the array is reached.
5. Repeat steps 1-4 n times, where n is the length of the array.
Here is the C++ code for bubble sorting an array in ascending order:
```
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
You can call this function by passing the array and its length as arguments:
```
int arr[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
bubbleSort(arr, n);
```

