To track and monitor real-time carbon dioxide emissions from a specific source, we can use sensors that measure the concentration of CO2 in the air. We can then use APIs to collect and analyze this data, taking into account factors like location, weather patterns, and human activities in the surrounding area.
Here's an example code that utilizes APIs and sensors to collect and analyze this data:
```swift
import UIKit
import CoreLocation
class ViewController: UIViewController, CLLocationManagerDelegate {
    let locationManager = CLLocationManager()
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        // Do any additional setup after loading the view.
        locationManager.delegate = self
        locationManager.requestWhenInUseAuthorization()
        locationManager.startUpdatingLocation()
    }
    func locationManager(_ manager: CLLocationManager, didUpdateLocations locations: [CLLocation]) {
        guard let location = locations.last else { return }
        let latitude = location.coordinate.latitude
        let longitude = location.coordinate.longitude
        let url = URL(string: "https://api.airvisual.com/v2/nearest_city?lat=\(latitude)&lon=\(longitude)&key=YOUR_API_KEY")!
        URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: url) { (data, response, error) in
            if let error = error {
                print(error.localizedDescription)
                return
            }
            guard let data = data else { return }
            do {
                let json = try JSONSerialization.jsonObject(with: data, options: []) as? [String: Any]
                let current = json?["data"] as? [String: Any]
                let pollution = current?["pollution"] as? [String: Any]
                let co2 = pollution?["co"] as? Int
                print("CO2 concentration: \(co2)")
            } catch {
                print(error.localizedDescription)
            }
        }.resume()
    }
}
```
In this code, we use the `CLLocationManager` to get the user's current location. We then use this location to call the AirVisual API to get the nearest city's air quality data. The API returns a JSON object that we parse to get the current CO2 concentration. We can then use this data to track and monitor real-time CO2 emissions from a specific source.
Note that you will need to replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your own AirVisual API key. Additionally, you may need to modify this code to integrate with sensors that measure CO2 concentration in real-time.

